---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CopyableCode from '../components/CopyableCode.astro';

const config = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "leji": { "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@leji-org/mcp"] }
  }
}`;

const claudeAdd = 'claude mcp add leji --scope user -- npx -y @leji-org/mcp';
const codexAdd = 'codex mcp add leji -- npx -y @leji-org/mcp';

const resources = [
   ['leji://spec/full', 'The complete specification, one document.'],
   ['leji://spec/{id}', 'One spec document, e.g. leji://spec/conformance.'],
   ['leji://schema/{name}', 'One JSON Schema, e.g. leji://schema/context-manifest.'],
   ['leji://cli/help', 'The leji CLI command and option reference.'],
];

const tools = [
   ['search_spec', 'Spec sections matching a query.'],
   ['fetch_spec_doc', 'One spec document (or the whole spec).'],
   ['fetch_schema', 'One JSON Schema.'],
   ['validate_manifest', 'Validate a leji.json supplied inline.'],
   ['validate_layer', 'Validate the context layer at a path.'],
   ['score_conformance', 'Claimed vs verified conformance level.'],
   ['explain_conformance', 'What is verified, and what the next level needs.'],
];
---

<Doc
   title="MCP server · Leji"
   description="Use Leji tooling from your AI agent: a local, read-only MCP server that serves the spec and schemas and checks a context layer on disk."
>
   <h1>MCP server</h1>
   <p class="sp-lede">
      Leji's tooling comes in two ways. The <a href="/cli/">CLI</a> is for you and your CI; the Model Context Protocol (MCP)
      server is for your agent.
   </p>
   <p>
      <code>@leji-org/mcp</code> is a local <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         >MCP</a
      > server that wraps the reference SDK. Your agent reads the spec and schemas, validates a context layer on disk, and
      scores its conformance, with no site fetch and no CLI shell-out. It runs locally over stdio, needs no network or auth,
      and exposes only read-only tools.
   </p>

   <h2>Add it to your client</h2>
   <p>
      Configure it once and the <code>leji</code> tools appear in every session its scope covers, with no further setup: a
      user-level registration reaches every project, a project-level one reaches that repository. The client launches{
         ' '
      }
      <code>leji-mcp</code> on demand.
   </p>
   <p>
      If you're adopting with the CLI and either Claude Code or Codex is on your <code>PATH</code>, <code
         >leji init</code
      >
      and <code>leji adopt</code> offer to register this server for that host, before handing off to your agent. The offer
      is interactive, so a <code>--yes</code> run skips it. What follows is for anyone who skipped the prompt, uses another
      client, or wants a different scope.
   </p>

   <h3>Claude Code</h3>
   <CopyableCode code={claudeAdd} lang="bash" label="Copy command" />
   <p>
      <code>--scope user</code> installs it once for your user, so the <code>leji</code> tools are available in every project;
      drop it to limit the server to the current project. Full reference:{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code MCP docs</a>.
   </p>

   <h3>Codex</h3>
   <CopyableCode code={codexAdd} lang="bash" label="Copy command" />
   <p>
      Or add an <code>[mcp_servers.leji]</code> table to <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> directly. Full reference:{
         ' '
      }
      <a href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Codex MCP docs</a>.
   </p>

   <h3>Other clients</h3>
   <p>Claude Desktop, Cursor, Windsurf, and any other MCP client take the standard config:</p>
   <CopyableCode code={config} lang="json" label="Copy MCP config" />

   <p>
      Requires <a href="https://nodejs.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Node.js</a> 22 or newer (<code>npx</code> ships
      with Node). The first run fetches the package; later runs use the cache. Point each tool at the repository root that
      holds your <code>leji.json</code>.
   </p>

   <h2>Tools</h2>
   <p>All read-only. Nothing the server exposes can write or change your context layer.</p>
   <table class="mcp-table">
      <caption class="vh">MCP tools the server exposes</caption>
      <thead>
         <tr><th scope="col">Tool</th><th scope="col">What it returns</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
         {
            tools.map(([name, summary]) => (
               <tr>
                  <th scope="row">
                     <code>{name}</code>
                  </th>
                  <td>{summary}</td>
               </tr>
            ))
         }
      </tbody>
   </table>

   <h2>Resources</h2>
   <table class="mcp-table">
      <caption class="vh">MCP resources the server exposes</caption>
      <thead>
         <tr><th scope="col">URI</th><th scope="col">What it serves</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
         {
            resources.map(([uri, summary]) => (
               <tr>
                  <th scope="row">
                     <code>{uri}</code>
                  </th>
                  <td>{summary}</td>
               </tr>
            ))
         }
      </tbody>
   </table>

   <h2>Why a server, and not just the CLI?</h2>
   <p>
      The CLI already validates and scores a context layer. The server adds no new capability; it's a different surface,
      the one an agent reaches for on its own:
   </p>
   <ul>
      <li>
         <strong>It's self-advertised.</strong> Configured once, it shows up in your agent's tool list every session, with
         descriptions of exactly what it does. Nobody has to tell the agent that Leji exists or where to find the CLI.
      </li>
      <li>
         <strong>It works where a shell doesn't.</strong> Many agent environments can't or won't run arbitrary commands. A
         read-only server with no network is reachable where <code>leji validate</code> is not.
      </li>
      <li>
         <strong>It serves the spec and schemas directly.</strong> Your agent reads them as resources, without a web fetch,
         and cites them as it works.
      </li>
   </ul>
   <p>
      Consuming a context layer doesn't need the server: a conformant repository's <a href="/spec/boot-profile/"
         >boot profile</a
      > already onboards your agent. The server is for the other half, learning Leji and checking a context layer against
      it.
   </p>
</Doc>

<style>
   .mcp-table {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
   }
   .mcp-table caption.vh {
      position: absolute;
      width: 1px;
      height: 1px;
      overflow: hidden;
      clip-path: inset(50%);
      white-space: nowrap;
   }
   .mcp-table th {
      padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem 0.5rem 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
      vertical-align: top;
      text-align: left;
      font-weight: 400;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      white-space: nowrap;
   }
   .mcp-table thead th {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--blue);
   }
   .mcp-table th code {
      color: var(--blue-deep);
      font-size: 0.85em;
   }
   .mcp-table td {
      padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem 0.5rem 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
      vertical-align: top;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
   }
   .mcp-table td:first-child {
      white-space: nowrap;
   }
   .mcp-table td code {
      color: var(--blue-deep);
      font-size: 0.85em;
   }
</style>
